{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2169-03-25 21:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, I’m Dr. Lee. I’ll be taking care of you during this stay. You were brought in from rehab because of vomiting—can you tell me about that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2169-03-25 21:46:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I had two episodes this morning. The rehab staff got worried because it looked dark, almost like coffee grounds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2169-03-25 21:47:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. You’ve had gastroparesis for a while—has vomiting like this happened before?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2169-03-25 21:48:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I do vomit sometimes. It’s usually brownish. I didn’t feel any different this time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2169-03-25 21:48:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. You’re not having abdominal pain, nausea, or shortness of breath now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2169-03-25 21:49:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, I feel okay now. Just tired."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2169-03-25 21:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We checked your blood—your hemoglobin is stable at 7.7, which is your usual level. That’s reassuring. We also did a stool test, and it was negative for blood."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2169-03-25 21:51:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not a bleed?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2169-03-25 21:51:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not likely. The GI team thinks the dark color could be from irritation in your esophagus, maybe from your feeding tube. We don’t think you need an endoscopy."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2169-03-25 21:52:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That makes sense. I’ve had that tube for a while."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2169-03-25 21:53:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll keep monitoring you. We also noticed your troponin was slightly elevated. Did you have any chest pain?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2169-03-25 21:53:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, none at all."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2169-03-25 21:54:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. Your EKG doesn’t show any signs of a heart attack. The troponin is probably elevated because of your kidney issues, not a new heart problem."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2169-03-25 21:55:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m on dialysis, so that makes sense."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2169-03-25 21:55:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. We’ll continue your regular dialysis. You’re scheduled for it tomorrow at noon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2169-03-25 21:56:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, thank you."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2169-03-25 22:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We also reviewed your chest X-ray. It shows some fluid in your lungs, which we’ve seen before. No pneumonia or new issues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2169-03-25 22:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had that since my surgery."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2169-03-25 22:31:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, it’s chronic. We’ll manage it with dialysis and your heart meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2169-03-26 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs overnight were stable. No further vomiting, and your kidney function is consistent with baseline."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2169-03-26 08:01:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I haven’t vomited again. I did feel a little nauseous earlier, but it passed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2169-03-26 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re going to start you on Zofran for nausea, as needed. It might help prevent vomiting."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2169-03-26 08:02:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That sounds good. I’ve taken it before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2169-03-26 08:03:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We also checked a stool test for C. difficile, which came back negative. So no infection there."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2169-03-26 08:04:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good to know."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2169-03-26 12:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You just finished your dialysis. How are you feeling?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2169-03-26 12:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A bit tired, but okay. No nausea so far."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2169-03-26 12:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your blood pressure is stable. We’ll keep an eye on your volume status."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2169-03-26 15:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve updated your medications. You’re now on metoprolol, which helps your heart after bypass surgery."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2169-03-26 15:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I wasn’t on that before?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2169-03-26 15:01:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, but it’s standard after CABG. It protects your heart and helps with blood pressure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2169-03-26 15:02:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I trust you."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2169-03-27 07:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—we’re planning to discharge you back to rehab today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2169-03-27 07:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Really? That was fast."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2169-03-27 07:01:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. You’ve been stable, no more vomiting, and your labs haven’t changed. The team feels you’re safe to return."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2169-03-27 07:02:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about the warfarin? I remember my INR was high last time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2169-03-27 07:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good point. We held one dose because your INR was 3.2, but you’ll restart at your usual 3 mg daily. Cardiology will follow up on that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2169-03-27 07:04:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the Zofran?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2169-03-27 07:04:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You can take it as needed for nausea—4 mg every 8 hours if needed. Keep an eye on the vomiting and let rehab know if it happens again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2169-03-27 07:05:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you for explaining everything."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2169-03-27 07:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll send all the details to your rehab team. Take care."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2169-03-25 21:45:00",
    "admission_end": "2169-03-27 15:45:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with type 1 diabetes, gastroparesis, recent CABG and mitral valve replacement, and end-stage renal disease was admitted from rehab due to two episodes of brown vomiting, initially concerning for GI bleeding. She denied pain, nausea, or further vomiting. Labs showed stable hemoglobin and a negative stool test. Elevated troponin was attributed to chronic kidney disease, not acute coronary syndrome. A chest X-ray showed persistent pulmonary edema and pleural effusions. GI consultation found no need for endoscopy, suspecting tube-related irritation. She underwent scheduled hemodialysis, was started on metoprolol post-CABG, and given ondansetron for nausea. She remained stable, with no recurrent vomiting, and was discharged back to rehab with updated medications and follow-up plans.",
    "problems": [
      "Diabetes with neurological manifestations, type I [juvenile type], not stated as uncontrolled",
      "End stage renal disease",
      "Acute kidney failure with lesion of tubular necrosis",
      "Peripheral autonomic neuropathy in disorders classified elsewhere",
      "Hypertensive chronic kidney disease, unspecified, with chronic kidney disease stage V or end stage renal disease",
      "Gastroparesis",
      "Long-term (current) use of insulin",
      "Diabetes with ophthalmic manifestations, type I [juvenile type], not stated as uncontrolled",
